<h2 id="h-cam-system-and-draw-cycle-performance">Cam System and Draw Cycle Performance</h2>
<p>The Prima is built to feel smooth from the start of the draw to the valley, with a defined back wall that makes it easy to find the same anchor every time. An 80% let-off means you hold only a fraction of the peak weight at full draw, which helps when you have to wait for a deer to step clear.</p>

<h3 id="h-mathews-prima-mod-chart">Mathews Prima Mod Chart and Draw Length</h3>
<p>Draw length on the Prima is set with draw length mods (modules). The Mathews Prima mod chart lists which mod and position matches each draw length across the 21.5- to 27.5-inch range. Use the chart from Mathews or your dealer for your specific bow rather than a forum screenshot, and have a pro shop make the change if a bow press is needed.</p>
<p>Once the mod is set, the rest of the fit falls into place. Your peep should meet your eye at a natural head position, your D-loop should let your release hand settle into a repeatable anchor, and your bow arm should have a slight, relaxed bend. If any of those feel forced, revisit draw length before blaming the bow. A half-inch change in the wrong direction can make a well-designed bow feel awkward, while the right setting makes a short-draw bow like the Prima feel calm and steady at full draw. Shoot several dozen arrows at a new setting before deciding whether it is right.</p>
<?= callout('warn', 'Measure before you buy', 'Draw length decides whether the Prima fits you at all. If your measured draw is longer than 27.5 inches, the Prima is not the right bow. Use our draw length calculator, then confirm at a pro shop.') ?>

<h2 id="h-weight-balance-and-handling-characteristics">Weight, Balance, and Handling Characteristics</h2>
<p>At 3.93 pounds bare, the Prima is light, and a package will add weight once the sight, quiver and stabilizer are mounted. That is normal. The stabilizer helps balance the sight and quiver so the bow settles on target rather than tipping to one side. If the setup feels front-heavy, try shooting with the quiver removed before changing anything else.</p>

<h2 id="h-accuracy-and-forgiveness">Accuracy and Forgiveness</h2>
<p>A 5.5-inch brace height sits in the moderate range, which tends to forgive small form errors better than very short brace heights. Pair that with a correct draw length and a repeatable anchor and you have a bow that rewards good habits. Arrow choice matters too: have your dealer match spine to your draw weight and draw length.</p>

<h2 id="h-noise-vibration-and-shot-feel">Noise, Vibration, and Shot Feel</h2>
<p>Mathews has a long reputation for quiet, dead-in-the-hand bows, and the Prima continues that approach. A package stabilizer and properly installed string accessories help keep shot noise down. If you hear a buzz or rattle, check that every accessory screw is tight and the quiver is seated firmly.</p>

<h2 id="h-adjustability-and-longevity">Adjustability and Longevity</h2>
<p>The Prima is offered in 40, 50 and 60 lb peak weight options, and draw weight can be turned down from peak within the limb range. Combined with the mod system for draw length, that lets the bow adapt as you get stronger or as your form changes. Keep the string waxed, inspect cables and limbs before each season, and have a pro shop replace strings when they show wear.</p>

<h2 id="h-mathews-prima-year">Mathews Prima Year and Buying Tips</h2>
<p>The Mathews Prima was introduced for the 2021 model year, according to retailer listings. If you are shopping for a Mathews Prima for sale, new or used, keep these points in mind:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li>Buy through an authorized Mathews dealer when possible so you get setup support and warranty help.</li>
  <li>On a used bow, confirm the serial number, and inspect limbs, cams and the string.</li>
  <li>Confirm the draw length mod installed and the peak weight option (40, 50 or 60 lb).</li>
  <li>Check current price with local dealers; pricing changes with season and package contents.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-hunting-applications">Hunting Applications</h2>
<p>The Prima was designed as a hunting bow, and its compact size and light weight suit treestands, ground blinds and spot-and-stalk hunts. Kinetic energy depends on draw weight, draw length and arrow weight, so a shorter-draw shooter should pick an arrow heavy enough for the game being hunted. Check your setup with our kinetic energy calculator.</p>
<?= tool_cta('kinetic-energy-calculator') ?>
<p>Fixed-blade broadheads that fly well at moderate speeds are a sensible match. A sharp <?= amz('fixed blade broadheads 100 grain', 'fixed-blade broadhead') ?> and a good <?= amz('bag archery target broadhead', 'broadhead target') ?> for practice should be on your list before season.</p>

<h2 id="h-target-and-recreational-shooting">Target and Recreational Shooting</h2>
<p>The same traits that help in the field work on the range: a stable draw, a solid back wall and a forgiving brace height. For 3D shooting you might add a longer stabilizer or a single-pin sight later. The Prima is a hunting bow first, but it is plenty of fun in the backyard.</p>

